# Where DuckFleet deploys. The one place any of it is named.
#
# These are defaults, not constants: every one may be overridden from the
# environment, so pointing the lane at a DuckFleet-owned cluster is a change here
# rather than a change spread across scripts, a workflow and a chart.
#
# The values below still describe the shared entity-resolution development cluster,
# which is where this deploys today. That coupling is the subject of #12 and is not
# removed by making it configurable — it is only made removable. Capacity there is
# not ours to grant, which has already blocked two capabilities:
# entity-resolution-engine#1086 (2-CPU workers) and #39 (pod-create RBAC for isolated
# compute).

# The EKS cluster, and the local kubeconfig context that must point at it. The
# context is asserted against the cluster's real endpoint before anything is applied,
# so a stale or wrong context fails rather than deploying somewhere unintended.
DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER=${DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER:-er-dev}
DUCKFLEET_KUBE_CONTEXT=${DUCKFLEET_KUBE_CONTEXT:-er-dev-admin}

# The namespace DuckFleet owns on that cluster. Unlike the rest, this is ours.
DUCKFLEET_NAMESPACE=${DUCKFLEET_NAMESPACE:-duckfleet-dev}

# The EKS NodeClass the DuckFleet node pool draws its node configuration from — the
# node IAM role, and the subnet and security group selectors. Referenced by name rather
# than defined, because those are facts about a cluster this repository does not own and
# would otherwise have to restate and keep in step.
#
# `default` is what EKS Auto Mode creates. A cluster whose built-in node pools are
# disabled may name it something else; scripts/aws/apply-boundary.sh lists what exists
# if this does not, rather than applying a node pool that can never provision.
DUCKFLEET_NODE_CLASS=${DUCKFLEET_NODE_CLASS:-default}

DUCKFLEET_REGION=${DUCKFLEET_REGION:-us-east-2}
DUCKFLEET_ACCOUNT=${DUCKFLEET_ACCOUNT:-797781631727}

# The SSO profile an operator uses. Unset under GitHub Actions, where OIDC supplies
# short-lived environment credentials instead.
DUCKFLEET_AWS_PROFILE=${DUCKFLEET_AWS_PROFILE:-er-platform}

# Whether the namespace must carry the shared-platform bootstrap label. True on a
# cluster DuckFleet does not own, where the namespace is created for it by someone
# else and its absence means the bootstrap was never applied. A cluster DuckFleet
# owns creates its own namespace, so there is nothing to wait for.
DUCKFLEET_REQUIRE_BOOTSTRAP=${DUCKFLEET_REQUIRE_BOOTSTRAP:-true}

# Where Terraform keeps its state. Still an entity-resolution bucket: DuckFleet owns
# only the key within it, which is the dependency #12 removes. Changing this default
# is the last step of the migration, after scripts/aws/bootstrap-state.sh has created
# the replacement and scripts/aws/migrate-state.sh has copied the state into it — so
# the lane is never pointed at a bucket that is not ready.
DUCKFLEET_STATE_BUCKET=${DUCKFLEET_STATE_BUCKET:-er-terraform-state-797781631727}
DUCKFLEET_STATE_KEY=${DUCKFLEET_STATE_KEY:-duckfleet/dev.tfstate}

# The cluster module keeps its own state: it is applied by an operator, not by CI, so
# a mistake there cannot touch the state CI writes.
DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER_STATE_KEY=${DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER_STATE_KEY:-duckfleet/cluster.tfstate}
